			<div class="content" id="post-content-4502808">
									

<p>Read SCP-4382 a few months ago; enjoyed this one no less. I really like how this article both expounds the concept of Locked Scenarios <em>and</em> provides a no-frills glimpse into domestic dysfunction. :)</p>
<div class="collapsible-block">
<div class="collapsible-block-folded"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">+&nbsp;Couple&nbsp;of&nbsp;errors&nbsp;I&nbsp;noticed</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded" style="display:none">
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded-link"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">-&nbsp;Couple&nbsp;of&nbsp;errors&nbsp;I&nbsp;noticed</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-content">
<blockquote>
<p>What? Oh, no, no <em>this guy just</em> sitting in the seat next to the subject. Sa- 5-1 signaled me to take him out, so I followed him <em>into bathroom</em>. I tried to play nice, really, but this guy really, really wants to get back to his seat!</p>
</blockquote>
<p>Should it be &quot;just this guy&quot; and &quot;into <span style="text-decoration: underline;">the</span> bathroom&quot; respectively?</p>
<blockquote>
<p>(…) Copy, 5-4. Keep us appraised.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>apprised?</p>
<p>Another minor gripe, <em>A Streetcar Named Desire</em> in its original production comprised twelve <em>scenes</em>, and did not utilise act divisions. No biggie though!</p>
</div>
